# yagit's local configuration.
#
# `./do` creates .env from this file on first run, replacing __HOME__ with your
# real home directory. .env is never committed: it describes this machine and
# nothing else.
#
# Everything the project can decide for itself — ports, listen address, the
# session token — is set by `./do`. Only what follows is machine-specific.
#
# `./do` reads exactly YAGIT_ROOT, YAGIT_PUBLIC_URL, YAGIT_PUBLIC_HOST and
# YAGIT_LISTEN_ALL. Any other YAGIT_ assignment here is ignored, and `./do` says
# which.

# The root under which yagit is allowed to open repositories.
#
# This is a security boundary, not a convenience: the daemon refuses to open
# anything outside it, after resolving symlinks.
YAGIT_ROOT=__HOME__

# Leave everything below commented out when you develop and browse on the same
# machine. When the browser is somewhere else — a VM, a headless box you reach
# over SSH, a remote workstation — pick ONE of the two ways in that follow.

# 1. Through a reverse proxy on this machine. The recommended way.
#
# The address the proxy serves yagit at: https:// or http://, a host, a port
# only if it is not the scheme's own, and no path. A development VM that gives
# every app a host name of its own, with TLS in front, is this case.
#
# The daemon stays on the loopback (127.0.0.1:7420), which is where the proxy
# connects to it, so nothing listens on the network that was not already. Point
# the proxy at 127.0.0.1:7420, pass WebSocket upgrades through — hot reload
# rides one — leave responses unbuffered, since the event stream is one that
# never ends, and have it send X-Forwarded-Proto, so the session cookie is
# marked Secure when the browser's side is HTTPS. `./do` then prints this URL,
# and the daemon accepts writes from a page served there.
#
# Give yagit a host name of its own rather than a port on a shared one: a
# browser keeps cookies per host, not per port, so apps on one name and
# different ports read and overwrite each other's.
#
# Setting this beside a non-loopback YAGIT_PUBLIC_HOST, or beside
# YAGIT_LISTEN_ALL=1, is refused: the two ways in cannot both be meant.
#
#   YAGIT_PUBLIC_URL=https://yagit.my-dev-box.local

# 2. Straight to the daemon, by the machine's name.
#
# The hostname a browser uses to reach the daemon. Set YAGIT_LISTEN_ALL=1 below
# as well: `./do` widens the listen address only when both a non-loopback
# YAGIT_PUBLIC_HOST and YAGIT_LISTEN_ALL=1 are set. It then prints a URL you can
# paste into a browser as is.
#
#   YAGIT_PUBLIC_HOST=my-dev-box.local

# Acknowledge that the daemon will listen on all interfaces (0.0.0.0).
#
# Required when YAGIT_PUBLIC_HOST names a host other than the loopback.
# Without it, `./do` refuses to start rather than silently exposing the daemon
# to the network. The session token still guards every route — this flag only
# says you understand the listen address widens.
#
#   YAGIT_LISTEN_ALL=1

# TLS certificate and private key for HTTPS — the daemon's, not `./do`'s.
#
# When both are set, the daemon serves HTTPS and marks the session cookie
# Secure. Plain HTTP remains the default for local development, and behind a
# reverse proxy the proxy's TLS is the one that counts (see YAGIT_PUBLIC_URL).
#
# `./do` does not read them, and would go on probing the stack it started over
# http://. Setting them here does nothing, and `./do` names them on the way
# past rather than leaving you to wonder. They belong to a binary you run
# yourself:
#
#   YAGIT_TLS_CERT=cert.pem YAGIT_TLS_KEY=key.pem \
#     ./dist/yagit-linux-amd64 -root "$HOME"
